<div class="space-y-12">
    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="border-b border-white/10 pb-8">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight lg:text-5xl mb-4">
            <span class="text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-400 via-orange-400 to-amber-400 animate-gradient-x">
                OpenID Connect Provider
            </span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
            Be an identity provider, not just talk to one. One command scaffolds a working
            OpenID Connect provider so other applications can &ldquo;Sign in with your app&rdquo;.
        </p>
    </div>

    <section>
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli generate auth            # the provider signs tokens about a signed-in user
soli generate oidc_provider</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
            The second command refuses to run without the first: the provider needs a <code class="text-amber-300">User</code>
            model and the <code class="text-amber-300">current_user</code> middleware to know who is being authenticated.
            The generated provider implements the <strong class="text-white">Authorization Code flow with PKCE</strong> &mdash;
            the only flow OAuth&nbsp;2.1 still recommends.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Endpoints -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">1</span>
            Endpoints
        </h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10 text-left text-gray-300">
                        <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Route</th>
                        <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Purpose</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="text-gray-400">
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-amber-300">GET /.well-known/openid-configuration</code></td><td>Discovery document</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-amber-300">GET /.well-known/jwks.json</code></td><td>Public signing keys</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-amber-300">GET /oauth/authorize</code></td><td>Authorization + consent screen</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-amber-300">POST /oauth/authorize</code></td><td>Consent decision</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-amber-300">POST /oauth/token</code></td><td>Code, refresh and client-credentials grants</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-amber-300">GET|POST /oauth/userinfo</code></td><td>Claims for a bearer token</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-amber-300">POST /oauth/revoke</code></td><td>RFC 7009 revocation</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-amber-300">GET /oauth/logout</code></td><td>RP-initiated logout</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-4 mt-6">
            <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">
                The generated routes call <code class="text-amber-300">skip_csrf</code> on <code class="text-amber-300">/oauth/token</code>
                and <code class="text-amber-300">/oauth/revoke</code>. Those are server-to-server calls with no browser
                <code>Origin</code>, so the same-origin gate would reject every legitimate exchange; the <em>client</em> is
                authenticated instead. The consent <code>POST</code> is a real browser form and keeps CSRF protection.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Setup -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">2</span>
            Setup
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">Signing keys</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3 leading-relaxed">
            The generator deliberately does <strong class="text-white">not</strong> create keys &mdash; a private key written
            into <code class="text-amber-300">config/</code> ends up committed. Generate them yourself:
        </p>
<pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -out oidc.pem
openssl rsa -in oidc.pem -pubout -out oidc.pub.pem</code></pre>
<pre data-filename=".env"><code class="language-bash text-sm">SOLI_OIDC_ISSUER=https://id.example.com
SOLI_OIDC_PRIVATE_KEY="$(cat oidc.pem)"
SOLI_OIDC_PUBLIC_KEY="$(cat oidc.pub.pem)"</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-3 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
            Both halves are configured because signing needs the private PEM and JWKS publication needs the public one.
            <code class="text-amber-300">SOLI_OIDC_ISSUER</code> <strong class="text-white">must</strong> match your public
            origin exactly, with no trailing slash &mdash; relying parties compare the <code>iss</code> claim byte for byte. Unset, it
            is <code>http://localhost:5011</code> in development, and with <code>APP_ENV=production</code> every request that needs it
            fails rather than publish that issuer. It is never read from the <code>Host</code> header.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">Migrations</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
            <code class="text-amber-300">soli db:migrate up</code>. SoliDB creates a collection on first model access, so the
            migration exists for the <strong class="text-white">indexes</strong>. The unique ones are not an optimisation:
            <code class="text-amber-300">code_digest</code> unique is the database-level backstop that keeps an authorization
            code single-use even if the application-level burn ever raced.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">Register a relying party</h3>
<pre data-filename="Register a client"><code class="language-soli text-sm">result = OauthClient.register("My App", ["https://app.example/callback"], {})
print(result["client_id"])
print(result["client_secret"])   # shown once — only its Argon2 digest is stored</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-3 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
            A <strong class="text-white">public</strong> client (SPA, mobile) has no secret, so PKCE is forced on and cannot be
            switched off &mdash; it is the only thing binding the code to the requester.
            <code class="text-amber-300">post_logout_redirect_uris</code> (an option) lists where
            <code>/oauth/logout</code> may send the browser back to; left out, the client&rsquo;s
            <code>redirect_uris</code> serve, as they did before the option existed, and <code>[]</code> means nowhere. Every URI
            must be <code>https</code>, or plain <code>http</code> to this machine only (<code>localhost</code>, <code>127.0.0.1</code>,
            <code>[::1]</code>) &mdash; the rule dynamic registration applies.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">Record when the user signed in, and come back mid-flow</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-white">Every path that signs a user in</strong> records the moment, next to its
            <code>session_set("user_id", &hellip;)</code>. Apps generated after 2.39.3 already do it in each scaffold
            below, and impersonation already deletes it; in an app generated earlier, the generator does not modify
            files it did not create, so make these edits yourself:
        </p>
<pre data-filename="every sign-in path"><code class="language-soli text-sm">session_set("auth_time", DateTime.utc.to_unix)</code></pre>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-400 mt-3 mb-3 text-sm">
            <li>Password: <code>SessionsController#create</code>, next to <code>session_regenerate()</code></li>
            <li>Two-factor: <code>TwoFactorController#verify</code>, the code check that finishes the sign-in</li>
            <li>Sign-up: <code>RegistrationsController#create</code>; password reset: <code>PasswordsController#update</code> (both sign the user in)</li>
            <li>Passkey: <code>PasskeySessionsController#create</code>; magic link: <code>MagicLinksController#redeem</code>; SAML: <code>SamlController#acs</code></li>
            <li>OAuth provider: already done by <code>OauthSso.sign_in</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-white">Impersonation</strong> must <code class="text-amber-300">session_delete("auth_time")</code>
            when an admin starts and when they stop impersonating: the session then belongs to someone who did not sign in at
            that time. A remember-me cookie restoring a session is not a sign-in either: leave <code>auth_time</code> unset there.
            Then, in <code>SessionsController#create</code>, replace the final <code>return redirect("/")</code> with:
        </p>
<pre data-filename="app/controllers/sessions_controller.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">destination = session_get("oidc_return_to") ?? "/"
session_delete("oidc_return_to")
return redirect(destination)</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
            Without <code>auth_time</code> the provider does not know when the user signed in: the id_token carries no
            <code class="text-amber-300">auth_time</code> (it used to claim the authorization instant, which told a client that a
            day-old session was fresh), and a request with <code>max_age</code> is answered <code>login_required</code> &mdash; a
            missing value counts as unknown, never as now. Without the redirect edit, a user who signs in mid-flow lands on the
            home page instead of completing the authorization.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-white"><code>prompt</code> and <code>max_age</code>.</strong> <code>prompt=none</code> answers
            <code>login_required</code> when no one is signed in and <code>consent_required</code> when consent is missing, without
            showing a page. <code>prompt=consent</code> shows the consent screen even when consent was given before or the
            client has <code>skip_consent</code>. <code>max_age=N</code> is honoured from the recorded <code>auth_time</code>: an older (or
            unrecorded) sign-in is answered <code>login_required</code>, on the consent form&rsquo;s POST as on the GET, so a consent
            page left open issues no code once the sign-in is past <code>max_age</code>. <code>prompt=login</code> is always answered
            <code>login_required</code>: the provider cannot make the person sign in again on its own, and OIDC Core &sect;3.1.2.1
            prefers that error to silently ignoring the request.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Tokens -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">3</span>
            Tokens &amp; key rotation
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-white">Access tokens are signed JWTs</strong> (<code class="text-amber-300">typ: "at+jwt"</code>,
            RFC 9068), so a resource server verifies them offline against the JWKS with no call back to the provider. The cost
            is that revoking a grant kills the <em>refresh</em> token immediately while an already-issued access token lives out
            its TTL. That TTL is deliberately short (10 minutes) because it, not the denylist, is what bounds the exposure.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-white">Refresh tokens are opaque and rotate.</strong> Every use issues a new one and retires the
            old. Presenting a retired token means it leaked, so the entire family is revoked &mdash; the legitimate holder loses
            access too, which is correct, because the provider cannot tell the two apart. A refresh token is only issued when the
            client asked for <code class="text-amber-300">offline_access</code>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-white">A password reset ends the grants.</strong> An authorization code, a device approval and a
            refresh token each record the user&rsquo;s <code class="text-amber-300">session_version</code> when they are made; once
            that has moved &mdash; a password reset, an account taken over from an unconfirmed squatter, anything that signs every
            session out &mdash; the code exchange or device poll is refused <code>invalid_grant</code>, and so is the next refresh
            (its whole family revoked); introspection reports such a refresh token inactive. A refresh token minted from a grant
            carries the grant&rsquo;s version, never the user&rsquo;s current one. Rows written before the field existed count as
            version 0. Without this, a squatter&rsquo;s code issued minutes before the address&rsquo;s owner took the account over
            still turned into tokens for it.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
            The <code class="text-amber-300">id_token</code> carries <code>iss</code>, <code>sub</code>, <code>aud</code>,
            <code>exp</code>, <code>iat</code>, the echoed <code>nonce</code>, <code>auth_time</code> and <code>at_hash</code>.
            Scope-gated claims come from the <code class="text-amber-300">oidc_user_claims</code> hook &mdash; the one place that
            needs to know what a <code>User</code> looks like. <code>profile</code> releases <code>name</code> (and
            <code>preferred_username</code> from a <code>username</code> field, if any); the address is released only under
            <code>email</code> (<code>email</code>, <code>email_verified</code>), which the consent screen names separately.
        </p>
<pre data-filename="Rotating a signing key"><code class="language-bash text-sm"># 1. keep the outgoing key published
SOLI_OIDC_PREVIOUS_PUBLIC_KEY="$(cat old.pub.pem)"
# 2. promote the new pair, deploy
SOLI_OIDC_PRIVATE_KEY="$(cat new.pem)"
SOLI_OIDC_PUBLIC_KEY="$(cat new.pub.pem)"
# 3. once OIDC_ID_TOKEN_TTL has elapsed, unset the previous key</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
            The JWKS lists both keys during the overlap; signing always uses the active one alone. This only works because tokens
            carry a <code class="text-amber-300">kid</code> header.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Security -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">4</span>
            Security decisions
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
            These are worth knowing about, because several are places where a looser implementation would still appear to work.
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">An access token dies with what it was granted under</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">It carries the session version of its grant (<code>sv</code>) and its refresh-token family (<code>cid</code>), and <code>/oauth/userinfo</code> and <code>/oauth/introspect</code> refuse it once the user&rsquo;s sessions were ended (a password reset, a takeover, a SCIM deactivation), the account is gone or deactivated, or the family was revoked &mdash; a replayed code, a reused refresh token, <code>/oauth/revoke</code>. That reaches tokens issued without a refresh token too. A resource server verifying offline still sees it valid until <code>exp</code> (10 minutes): use introspection where immediate revocation matters.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">A revoked family stays revoked</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm"><code>revoke_chain</code> records the family before it revokes its rows, and a refresh token minted while that happens is born revoked &mdash; a rotation in flight does not outlive the revocation.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">A logout ends the remembered session too</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm"><code>/oauth/logout</code> forgets the remember-me token and expires its cookie, as <code>DELETE /logout</code> does; otherwise the next request restored the session and the relying party&rsquo;s &ldquo;sign in&rdquo; found the user still there.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">An impersonating admin grants nothing</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm"><code>/oauth/authorize</code> and <code>/oauth/device</code> refuse while the session is an impersonation: the consent and the refresh token would outlive it, at a third party, in the user&rsquo;s name.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">Device codes</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">A user code names one pending request (a unique index; a taken code is drawn again), the approval updates that request by its key, and a poll writes only its own two fields &mdash; it cannot put a decided request back to pending or un-consume one.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">A refresh-token family has a ceiling</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Rotation used to buy 30 more days each time, for ever. A family now ends <code>OIDC_REFRESH_CHAIN_TTL</code> (90 days) after its first token, or when the sign-in it came from does (a SAML session&rsquo;s expiry) if that is sooner: a token never outlives the ceiling, and past it the client sends the user through <code>/oauth/authorize</code> again.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">A code and a refresh token belong to one client</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">The token endpoint looks at whose code or refresh token it was handed before it does anything with it. Another client presenting one gets <code>invalid_grant</code> and changes nothing &mdash; it cannot burn a code it intercepted, nor, by replaying a used code or a rotated token, revoke the tokens of the client it belongs to. A client whose <code>grant_types</code> lost <code>refresh_token</code> can no longer refresh.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">PKCE for every public client</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">A public client has no secret, so PKCE is what binds a code to it. It is required whatever the stored <code>require_pkce</code> flag says; the flag only matters for confidential clients.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">No credential in the URL</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm"><code>/oauth/token</code>, <code>/oauth/revoke</code>, <code>/oauth/introspect</code> and <code>/oauth/device_authorization</code> answer <code>invalid_request</code> to a <code>client_secret</code>, <code>code</code>, <code>code_verifier</code>, <code>refresh_token</code>, <code>device_code</code> or <code>token</code> sent in the query string: it would work, and stay in access logs, proxies and histories.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2"><code>/oauth/revoke</code> revokes access tokens too</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Handed one of its own access tokens, a client gets it denylisted by <code>jti</code> &mdash; userinfo and introspection refuse it from then on. It used to answer <code>200</code> and leave the token valid.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2"><code>iss</code> in the authorization response</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Every redirect back to a client &mdash; a code or an error &mdash; carries <code>iss</code>, and discovery says so (<code>authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported</code>, RFC 9207): a client talking to several providers can tell which one answered.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">A client with a request-object key signs every request</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Registering <code>request_object_key</code> used to make signed requests possible, not required: plain parameters still worked, so the signature protected nothing. Such a client now gets an error page without <code>request=</code>, and a request object is refused when it expires more than ten minutes ahead. The consent form of a signed request carries only a reference: what the signature covered is kept in the session for ten minutes and read back from there, so the form cannot change it and a short-lived object does not expire under the person reading the page.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">Smaller things</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm"><code>SOLI_OIDC_REGISTRATION_TOKEN</code> under 32 characters is ignored (registration answers <code>404</code>). An access token signed with the previous key verifies until it expires, as an id_token hint already did. The logout confirmation names the application asking and where the browser goes next. Outside production, an unset <code>SOLI_OIDC_ISSUER</code> falls back to <code>APP_BASE_URL</code> before <code>localhost</code>. Device requests an hour past their expiry are swept as new ones start. A code whose sign-in has ended by the time it is exchanged (a SAML session&rsquo;s expiry) issues nothing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">Throttled</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm"><code>/oauth/token</code>, <code>/oauth/introspect</code>, <code>/oauth/revoke</code> and registration share <code>OIDC_REQUESTS_PER_IP</code> (300 a minute) per address &mdash; each verifies a client secret, an Argon2 hash, for anyone naming a client id; starting a device flow has <code>OIDC_DEVICE_STARTS_PER_IP</code> (30), and typing user codes <code>OIDC_USER_CODE_ATTEMPTS_PER_IP</code> (20). The constants are in <code>app/services/oidc_config.sl</code>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">Exact <code class="text-amber-300">redirect_uri</code> matching</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Byte for byte against a registered URI &mdash; no prefix matching, no wildcards, no normalization. Anything looser lets an attacker who can register a lookalike path harvest authorization codes.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-red-500/10 border border-red-500/20 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-red-400 mb-2">Two errors never redirect</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm">An unknown <code class="text-amber-300">client_id</code> or an unregistered <code class="text-amber-300">redirect_uri</code> renders a 400 page instead of bouncing the browser. Redirecting on an unvalidated <code>redirect_uri</code> <em>is</em> the open redirect that RFC 6749 &sect;4.1.2.1 carves these two cases out to prevent. Every other authorization error does redirect, with <code>error</code>, <code>error_description</code> and the echoed <code>state</code>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">PKCE <code class="text-amber-300">S256</code> only</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm"><code>plain</code> is rejected for every client type. It offers no protection against an attacker who intercepted the authorization request, and accepting it only adds a downgrade path.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">Codes are single-use and bound</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">60-second TTL, tied to <code>client_id</code>, <code>redirect_uri</code> and <code>code_challenge</code> &mdash; all re-checked at exchange. The burn is a single atomic statement, so two simultaneous exchanges cannot both win. A replayed code revokes every token the first exchange produced.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">Client authentication</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm"><code>client_secret_basic</code> or <code>client_secret_post</code>, never both (RFC 6749 &sect;2.3). Secrets are Argon2 digests. A failed <em>Basic</em> authentication returns 401 with <code>WWW-Authenticate</code>; the same failure via form parameters returns 400 &mdash; a distinction from RFC 6749 &sect;5.2 that is routinely gotten wrong.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">Token responses are <code class="text-amber-300">Cache-Control: no-store</code></h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">They carry bearer credentials and must not sit in a proxy cache. This is why the generated code returns a raw response hash rather than <code>render_json</code>, whose fast-path headers cannot be extended.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-white mb-2">Logout asks unless the request proves itself</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">A bare <code>GET /oauth/logout</code> shows a confirmation page that posts back (CSRF-checked). The page is skipped, and <code>post_logout_redirect_uri</code> honoured, only for an <code>id_token_hint</code> this provider signed &mdash; verified with <code>jwt_verify</code> (RS256, the active or previous key, your issuer, a year of leeway on the expiry since RP-Initiated Logout allows an expired hint) &mdash; that names the user signed in here. An access token is not a hint. The hint used to be read with <code>jwt_decode_unsafe</code>, so anyone could forge one and skip the confirmation. With no one signed in there is nothing to end: the visitor gets a &ldquo;You are signed out&rdquo; page with a link back instead of an immediate redirect, which would let anyone holding an id_token bounce visitors through this endpoint. The return URI must be one of the client&rsquo;s <code>post_logout_redirect_uris</code> (or, without that field, its <code>redirect_uris</code>).</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Caveats -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">5</span>
            Caveats &amp; what is not implemented
        </h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-400 text-sm leading-relaxed list-disc list-inside">
            <li>Registered <code class="text-amber-300">redirect_uris</code> must be absolute <code>http</code>/<code>https</code> with no fragment. Custom-scheme native redirects (<code>com.example.app:/cb</code>) are rejected at registration, because the redirect helper only accepts http(s); supporting them would mean bypassing the guard that closes the open redirect.</li>
            <li>An app serving files from <code class="text-amber-300">public/.well-known/</code> (ACME HTTP-01, for example) can shadow the discovery document, since static files take precedence for <code>GET</code>.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Token introspection (RFC 7662):</strong> <code>POST /oauth/introspect</code> answers <code>{"active": true, &hellip;}</code> for a live token and <code>{"active": false}</code> for anything else &mdash; including a refresh token whose user&rsquo;s session version moved since it was issued. Confidential clients only, and each introspects only the tokens issued to it; a resource server that checks other clients&rsquo; tokens is registered by an admin with <code>"introspect_any": true</code>.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Dynamic registration (RFC 7591):</strong> off until <code>SOLI_OIDC_REGISTRATION_TOKEN</code> is set; that value is the initial access token. Registrants cannot request <code>client_credentials</code>, the device grant, <code>skip_consent</code>, <code>introspect_any</code> or scopes outside <code>OIDC_SUPPORTED_SCOPES</code>. Redirect URIs (and the optional <code>post_logout_redirect_uris</code>) must be <code>https</code>, or <code>http</code> to <code>127.0.0.1</code>, <code>[::1]</code> or <code>localhost</code> only (RFC 8252 &sect;7.3) &mdash; the whole host: after it only <code>:port</code> and a path or query, and no backslash anywhere, so <code>http://localhost.evil.example/</code>, <code>http://[::1]evil.example/</code> and <code>http://localhost:80\evil.example</code> are refused.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Device grant (RFC 8628):</strong> opt a client in with <code>urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:device_code</code>. The device gets a device code and a <code>WDJB-MJHT</code>-style user code from <code>/oauth/device_authorization</code>, the person approves at <code>/oauth/device</code>, and the device polls the token endpoint (<code>authorization_pending</code>, <code>slow_down</code>, <code>access_denied</code>, <code>expired_token</code>). A device code yields tokens exactly once.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Signed request objects (RFC 9101):</strong> register the client&rsquo;s public PEM as <code>request_object_key</code>; its <code>request=&lt;jwt&gt;</code> claims replace the query parameters. <code>request_uri</code> is not supported.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Machine-to-machine:</strong> add <code>"client_credentials"</code> to a confidential client's <code>grant_types</code> (off by default) and it can request a token with <code>grant_type=client_credentials</code>. The token's <code>sub</code> is the client, with <code>gty: "client-credentials"</code>; there is no id token or refresh token, <code>openid</code> and <code>offline_access</code> are refused, and userinfo answers 401.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Not implemented:</strong> front/back-channel logout, DPoP or mTLS sender constraining, and <code>request_uri</code>.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Implicit and hybrid flows are left out on purpose.</strong> They return tokens in the redirect URL, where history, logs and referrers keep them; OAuth 2.1 removes them and the code flow with PKCE covers every case they served.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</div>
